these are pictures of people who have  arrived late to their exam and have been  locked out they might have only been a  few minutes late but this is brazil's  national high school exam  enem  and at the same time all across the  country the exam starts and the gates  are locked and by the looks of this if  you're not there on time they're really  not letting you in  this footage is heartbreaking because  the exam is so important it only takes  place once per year and it is used for  entry into many of the brazilian  universities in 2016 there are 8.6  million candidates which makes it the  second largest exam in the world behind  china's gao cow it doesn't come without  controversy this year the exam took  place despite the pandemic causing  extensive school closures and hardships  for many students in fact this year  about 50 of registered candidates failed  to show up and only a month ago 37  workers resigned from the agency that  prepares the exam amidst claims of  political censorship of some of the  questions  thank you to everyone who requested i  take a look at this exam some have even  been waiting two years for me to get  around to it one person said they  thought it was harder than the sat but  easier than exams in asian countries and  i do tend to agree with that at least  the countries that i've looked at in  previous videos i've got a copy of the  2021 exam here to take a look at in  total there are 180 questions taken over  two days lasting a total of ten and a  half hours which is huge  it covers languages social science  natural science and mathematics  i've got here the natural science and  math paper  for this there are 45 questions on the  sciences that's chemistry physics and  biology and then another 45 questions  for math for this paper you would have  five hours so that works out to just  over three minutes per question the  questions are all multi-choice but if  you take a look at them they're  extremely wordy and very long and sort  of set up now this is all in portuguese  but i've also run the exam through a  translator and here is the english  version some things have been lost in  translation but i think it gives a  pretty good idea of what the exam is  about you might also notice that some of  the questions are pretty visual this one  here is from the pretty famous comic  calvin and hobbes and it looks like the  boy is complaining that it's hot but the  tiger says that what really bothers them  is the humidity  the question then asks why that might be  the case  with the answer being that sweat has  greater difficulty in evaporating from  the body when it's humid let's have a go  at question 115. again there's a really  long lead up to even know what the  question is about but it says that in  autumn the leaves of trees change color  from green to shades of yellow brown  orange and red the green color of the  leaves is due to the pigment chlorophyll  in plants with deciduous leaves the  production of chlorophyll decreases and  the green tone fades allowing other  pigments such as carotene with a yellow  orange coloration  and anthocyanin with a reddish tone to  dominate the tones of the leaves the  observed coloration is due to the  interaction of these pigments with solar  radiation as shown in the absorption  spectrum the chlorophyll molecules  absorb solar radiation in the blue and  red regions so the light reflected by  the leaves lacks these two tones and we  see them as green color  anthocyanins absorb light from the blue  to the green so in this case the light  reflected by the leaves contains the  complementary colours which are red and  orange  all of that is to ask in what range of  the visible spectrum  do the carotenes absorb the most  what they've told us about the carotenes  is that those leaves are yellow orange  in colour  so if we look at the little colour wheel  we've got yellow and orange here  the complementary colors to those are  violet and blue  so those are the correct answer a  while these questions may not be as  rigorous as some of the other ones we've  seen  you still need a very broad base of  knowledge because you're doing  everything from writing essays to  answering these questions about the  planets  you might even learn a thing or two  while you're in the exam here's question  135 it tells us about the mimetic  octopus which has very curious color  patterns and behaviors it often changes  the orientation of its tentacles  resembling some other animals  here you can see the octopus mimicking  allura fish that's what a lure fish  looks like here it is mimicking a  lionfish and here it is mimicking a sea  snake the question asks from an  evolutionary point of view the capacity  presented was established because  octopuses what with the correct answer  being because they survived the  selective pressures with these behaviors  here's the start of the math section  again very wordy even for math questions  the amount of reading that's involved  means the test can become very tiring to  take especially after five hours of this  here's a question that jumps out as  being interesting it looks like a  tesseract a four-dimensional cube  the question says many toys that are  often found in public squares and parks  are shaped like two and  three-dimensional geometric figures a  company was hired to develop a new form  of toy the proposal presented by the  company was a structure formed only by  metal rods connected to each other as  shown in the figure but it turns out you  don't actually need to know anything  about four dimensional cubes because the  question just asks based on the proposal  presented how many plain geometric  figures of each type are formed by the  union of the rods so basically take a  good look at this picture and see how  many shapes are in there luckily the  first answer turns out to be the correct  one see if you can spot 12 isosceles  trapezoids and 12 squares here we have  the cutest little mug i've ever seen in  an exam with the question asking us to  find the volume of the shape here  now this is actually called a truncated  cone or a frustum and there is a formula  for finding the volume of one of these  so if you show up with that memorized  it's probably going to be a bit easier  for you but if you're left high and dry  and don't know the formula it's not  impossible to work it out from scratch  as well what you do is you imagine  extending this to become a  complete cone  and then you find the volume of this  large cone and subtract off the volume  of this little cone here doing that and  using 3 as an approximation for pi  you'll get 732 as the answer  they have this very intricate and very  brazilian question here about all of the  football teams the question had been  asking something about how many  combinations you could make if you  arranged all of these team logos on a  shelf with a set of intricate conditions  but in the end this question was  nullified because i think they couldn't  totally agree on what the correct answer  would be so all this work kind of was  for nothing  the second to last question 179 looks  best formatted in the portuguese version  and it's about the golden ratio it talks  about how if you have a line divided  into two parts then those two parts will  be in golden proportion  when the ratio between the whole and the  largest one of the parts is the same as  the ratio between the larger part and  the smaller part you can also find the  golden ratio as the solution to the  equation phi squared is equal to 5 plus  1  and that becomes the first element of  this table here it then goes on to say  that phi cubed can be found as 2 phi  plus 1 and there are similar relations  for the rest of the powers the question  is to find what would phi to the 7 be  equal to  i guess you don't need to understand the  context too much but if you just look at  the table and try to find a pattern that  will give you this missing box you might  notice the fibonacci numbers that the  coefficient out here in front of the phi  is the previous two added together 5  plus 3 gives you  two plus three gives you five one plus  two gives you three so with that we'd  have eight plus five is thirteen and the  fibonacci numbers are also in the second  term  one one two three five eight the golden  ratio and the fibonacci numbers are  interlinked with each other in so many  weird ways but if you didn't already  know that then good luck noticing it for  yourself during the pressure of the exam  the very last question is basically an  essay that describes this sculpture here  in brussels called the atomium  it tries to describe that the whole  thing is a giant cube tilted onto one  vertex which is touching the ground and  you can take an elevator straight up  from the sphere through the middle one  through to one at the top i found this  really hard to visualize given that you  can't really see half of it in this  picture here but the question says that  we have a visitor who moves around the  interior of the sculpture always  following the shortest path between  vertices and passing all edges and all  diagonals of the cube  they want to know what the path of this  traveler would look like if it was  projected down onto the ground the  question does say that there are nine of  these metal spheres  and since you're traveling to each one  of them you would expect i guess nine  distinct points on the projection  although this first part of the journey  up the elevator from the ground to the  middle to the top all three of those  spheres are in the same line so it would  only appear as one in total then we  would expect seven dots on the  projection  one two three four five six seven means  that e is the correct answer here it's  also got the right amount of symmetry  compared to c which would be more of a  rectangular sculpture i happen to have  seen a bit of a brazilian tv show called  three percent and it's this futuristic  dystopia where most people live in this  impoverished inland  and when everyone is 20 years old they  get a single opportunity to complete  something called the process which is  essentially a big test and only three  percent of candidates pass this test and  get to go and live luxurious affluent  lives in this distant offshore society  in the show passing the process is  basically your only chance at a good  life and so people spend their entire  lives up until they're 20 preparing for  this one shot  it's a fictional story that speaks to a  lot more social issues like inequality  but i can't help but notice a lot of  parallels between that show and this  exam  it's this idea that you spend so much of  your life preparing for one single test  which can determine so much of your  future  this article i found from inside higher  ed spoke about how in 2016 there were 8  million students who were competing for  approximately 250 000 places in the  federal universities and when you crunch  the numbers on this it works out to  around three percent the same number  that make it through in the show  in both cases whether you're talking  about the physical process that they go  through on tv  or this intellectual exam both are  supposed to give an equal chance to  everyone they're supposed to admit the  three percent most deserving candidates  but of course there's always issues  associated with that goal for one in  education private coaching and access to  better schools and teachers will always  be easier for people that start off from  a more privileged position but for many  countries not just brazil where you've  got huge numbers of students applying  for relatively small amount of places  then you need to figure out some way of  doing things and these giant exams seem  to be currently one of the best  strategies that we have i suppose as a  student you just have to do the best you  can to be prepared  and to not be late  i'd like to thank brilliant for  sponsoring this video if you're looking  for a last minute gift idea that doesn't  require postage then gifting someone a  brilliant membership may help them to  discover an interest in science and math  brilliant is an interactive stem  learning platform that helps you to  learn concepts by working through them  in visual hands-on ways  these are some clips from their recently  updated logic course that develops a  basis for mathematical reasoning  you use limited information to make  predictions and to uncover the truth  if you use my link which is  brilliant.org slash tibi's then you can  get 20 off an annual subscription and  this also applies for gifted memberships  so i hope that whoever receives it  enjoys learning something new  a big thank you to brilliant and thank  you to my patreon supporters for making  this video possible  a special shout out to today's rather  dog looking patron cat of the day peppa  [Music]

